"Some matrices associated with the split (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=d5c07b8138bafca03592e0710bdb8d8ad1cfd2a9&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn0search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Joohyung Kim ... Distance-regular graph, Terwilliger algebra, split decomposition, q-tetrahedron\nSome matrices associated with the split\ndecomposition for a Q-polynomial\ndistance-regular graph\nJoohyung Kim\nAbstract\nWe consider a Q-polynomial distance-regular graph Γ with vertex set X and diam-\neter D ≥3. For µ, ν ∈{↓, ↑} we deﬁne a direct sum decomposition of the standard\nmodule V = CX, called the (µ, ν)–split decomposition. For this decomposition we\ncompute the complex conjugate and transpose of the associated primitive idempotents.\nNow ﬁx b, β ∈C such that b ̸= 1 and assume Γ has classical parameters (D, b, α, β)\nwith α = b −1. Under this assumption Ito and Terwilliger displayed an action of the\nq-tetrahedron algebra ⊠q on the standard module of Γ. To describe this action they\ndeﬁned eight matrices in MatX(C), called\nA,\nA∗,\nB,\nB∗,\nK,\nK∗,\nΦ,\nΨ.\nFor each matrix in the above list we compute the transpose and complex conjugate.\nUsing this information we compute the transpose and complex conjugate for each\ngenerator of ⊠q on V .\nKeywords. Distance-regular graph, Terwilliger algebra, split decomposition, q-tetrahedron\nalgebra.\n2000 Mathematics Subject Classiﬁcation.\nPrimary 05E30; Secondary 05E35,\n05C50\n1\nIntroduction\narXiv:0710.4383v1  [math.CO]  24 Oct 2007\nWe consider a Q-polynomial distance-regular graph Γ with vertex set X and diameter D ≥3\n(see Section 2 for formal deﬁnitions). Let V denote the vector space over C consisting of\ncolumn vectors whose coordinates are indexed by X and whose entries are in C. We call V\nthe standard module. For µ, ν ∈{↓, ↑} we deﬁne a direct sum decomposition of V called the\n(µ, ν)–split decomposition. In this decomposition the components are indexed by ordered\npairs of integers (i, j) (0 ≤i, j ≤D). For 0 ≤i, j ≤D we let Eµν\ni,j denote the projection\nonto component (i, j) of the decomposition. We show that Eµν\ni,j is real. We show that the\ntranspose (E↓↓\ni,j)t = E↑↑\nD−i,D−j and (E↓↑\ni,j)t = E↑↓\nD−i,D−j. Now ﬁx b, β ∈C such that b ̸= 1 and\nassume Γ has classical parameters (D, b, α, β) with α = b −1. Fix q ∈C such that b = q2.\nUnder this assumption Ito and Terwilliger displayed an action of the q-tetrahedron algebra\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSome matrices associated with the split decomposition for a Q-polynomial distance-regular graph (https://arxiv.org/abs/0710.4383)\nciteturn0academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 19.1 years ago; Title: Some matrices associated with the split decomposition for a Q-polynomial distance-regular graph\nAuthors: Joohyung Kim ... Under this assumption Ito and Terwilliger displayed an action of the $q$-tetrahedron algebra $\\boxtimes_q$ on the standard module of $\\Gamma$.\nTitle: Some matrices associated with the split decomposition for a Q-polynomial distance-regular graph\nAuthors: Joohyung Kim\nDate: Wed Oct 24 06:47:45 2007\n\nWe consider a $Q$-polynomial distance-regular graph $\\Gamma$ with vertex set $X$ and diameter $D \\geq 3$. For $\\mu, \\nu \\in \\lbrace \\downarrow, \\uparrow \\rbrace$ we define a direct sum decomposition of the standard module $V=\\C X$, called the $(\\mu,\\nu)$--split decomposition. For this decomposition we compute the complex conjugate and transpose of the associated primitive idempotents. Now fix $b,\\beta \\in \\mathbb C$ such that $b \\neq 1$ and assume $\\Gamma$ has classical parameters $(D,b,\\alpha,\\beta)$ with $\\alpha = b-1$. Under this assumption Ito and Terwilliger displayed an action of the $q$-tetrahedron algebra $\\boxtimes_q$ on the standard module of $\\Gamma$. To describe this action they defined eight matrices in $\\hbox{Mat}_X(\\mathbb C)$, called \\begin{eqnarray*} \\label{eq:list} A,\\quad A^*,\\quad B,\\quad B^*, \\quad K,\\quad K^*,\\quad \\Phi,\\quad \\Psi. \\end{eqnarray*} For each matrix in the above list we compute the transpose and complex conjugate. Using this information we compute the transpose and complex conjugate for each generator of $\\boxtimes_q$ on $V$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: Distance-regular graphs and the q-tetrahedron algebra. (https://dblp1.uni-trier.de/rec/journals/ejc/ItoT09.html)\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; # \"Distance-regular graphs and the q-tetrahedron algebra.\"\n\n# \"Distance-regular graphs and the q-tetrahedron algebra.\"\n\n  * help us\n\n    * How can I correct errors in dblp?\n    * contact dblp\n\nTatsuro Ito, Paul M. Terwilliger (2009)\n\n\n## Details and statistics\n\n  * show external API response\n\n    * JSON @ openalex.org\n\nsee also:\n\n    * API doc @ openalex.org\n\nDOI: 10.1016/J.EJC.2008.07.011\n\naccess: openImage\n\ntype: Journal Article\n\nmetadata version: 2021-02-12\n\n  * Image: Journal Articles\n\n    * view\n\n      * electronic edition via DOI (open access)\n\nauthority control:\n\n      *  \n\n    * export record\n\n      * BibTeX\n      * RIS\n      * RDF N-Triples\n      * RDF Turtle\n      * RDF/XML\n      * XML\n      * plain text\n\ndblp key:\n\n      * journals/ejc/ItoT09\n\n    * ask others\n\n      * Google\n      * Google Scholar\n      * Semantic Scholar\n      * Internet Archive Scholar\n      * CiteSeerX\n      * PubPeer\n\n    * share record\n\n      * Bluesky\n      * Reddit\n      * BibSonomy\n      * LinkedIn\n\npersistent URL:\n\n      * https://dblp.org/rec/journals/ejc/ItoT09\n\nTatsuro Ito, Paul M. Terwilliger:\nDistance-regular graphs and the q-tetrahedron algebra. Eur. J. Comb. 30(3): 682-697 (2009)\n\n\nlast updated on 2021-02-12 13:40 CET by the dblp team\n\n all metadata released as open data under CC0 1.0 license--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDistance-regular graphs and the $q$-tetrahedron algebra (https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0608694)\nciteturn0academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 20.2 years ago; Title: Distance-regular graphs and the $q$-tetrahedron algebra ... Let $\\Gamma$ denote a distance-regular graph with classical parameters $(D,b,\\alpha,\\beta)$ and $b\\not=1$, $\\alpha=b-1$.\nTitle: Distance-regular graphs and the $q$-tetrahedron algebra\nAuthors: Tatsuro Ito, Paul Terwilliger\nDate: Mon Aug 28 16:53:26 2006\n\nLet $\\Gamma$ denote a distance-regular graph with classical parameters $(D,b,\\alpha,\\beta)$ and $b\\not=1$, $\\alpha=b-1$. The condition on $\\alpha$ implies that $\\Gamma$ is formally self-dual. For $b=q^2$ we use the adjacency matrix and dual adjacency matrix to obtain an action of the $q$-tetrahedron algebra $\\boxtimes_q$ on the standard module of $\\Gamma$. We describe four algebra homomorphisms into $\\boxtimes_q$ from the quantum affine algebra $U_q({\\hat{\\mathfrak{sl}}_2})$; using these we pull back the above $\\boxtimes_q$-action to obtain four actions of $U_q({\\hat{\\mathfrak{sl}}_2})$ on the standard module of $\\Gamma$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDistance-regular graphs and the q-tetrahedron (https://kanazawa-u.repo.nii.ac.jp/record/13248/files/SC-PR-ITO-T-682.pdf)\nciteturn0search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; the q-tetrahedron algebra ... Let Γ denote a distance-regular graph with classical parameters (D, b, α, β) and ... Tetrahedron algebra, distance-regular graph, quantum aﬃne algebra,\nDistance-regular graphs and\nthe q-tetrahedron algebra\nTatsuro Ito∗† and Paul Terwilliger‡\nIn honor of Eiichi Bannai on his 60th Birthday\nAbstract\nLet Γ denote a distance-regular graph with classical parameters (D, b, α, β) and\nb ̸= 1, α = b −1.\nThe condition on α implies that Γ is formally self-dual.\nFor\nb = q2 we use the adjacency matrix and dual adjacency matrix to obtain an action of\nthe q-tetrahedron algebra ⊠q on the standard module of Γ. We describe four algebra\nhomomorphisms into ⊠q from the quantum aﬃne algebra Uq(bsl2); using these we pull\nback the above ⊠q-action to obtain four actions of Uq(bsl2) on the standard module of\nΓ.\nKeywords.\nTetrahedron algebra, distance-regular graph, quantum aﬃne algebra,\ntridiagonal pair.\n2000 Mathematics Subject Classiﬁcation. Primary: 05E30. Secondary: 05E35;\n17B37.\n1\nIntroduction\nIn [25] B. Hartwig and the second author gave a presentation of the three-point sl2 loop\nalgebra via generators and relations. To obtain this presentation they deﬁned a Lie algebra\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDistance-regular graphs of q-racah type and the (https://kanazawa-u.repo.nii.ac.jp/record/11027/files/SC-PR-ITO-T-241.pdf)\nciteturn0search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; distance-regular graph Γ, focussing on the Q-polynomial property and the subconstituent ... In Section 5 we discuss the split decomposition of Γ. ... The q-tetrahedron algebra ⊠q\nsequence {θi}D\ni=0 and dual eigenvalue sequence {θ∗\ni }D\ni=0 that satisfy\nθi\n=\nη + uq2i−D + vqD−2i\n(0 ≤i ≤D),\nθ∗\ni\n=\nη∗+ u∗q2i−D + v∗qD−2i\n(0 ≤i ≤D),\nwhere q, u, v, u∗, v∗are nonzero and q2i ̸= 1 for 1 ≤i ≤D. Assume Γ has q-Racah type.\nFix a vertex x of Γ and let T = T(x) denote the corresponding subconstituent algebra [32,\nDeﬁnition 3.3]. Recall that T is generated by the adjacency matrix A and the dual adjacency\nmatrix A∗= A∗(x) [32, Deﬁnition 3.10]. An irreducible T-module W is called thin whenever\nthe intersection of W with each eigenspace of A and each eigenspace of A∗has dimension at\nmost 1 [32, Deﬁnition 3.5]. Assuming each irreducible T-module is thin, we display invertible\ncentral elements Φ, Ψ of T and a homomorphism ϑ : ⊠q →T such that\nA\n=\nηI + uΦΨ−1ϑ(x01) + vΨΦ−1ϑ(x12),\nA∗\n=\nη∗I + u∗ΦΨϑ(x23) + vΨ−1Φ−1ϑ(x30),\nwhere the xij are the standard generators of ⊠q. It follows that T is generated by the image\nϑ(⊠q) together with Φ, Ψ. In particular T is generated by ϑ(⊠q) together with the center\nZ(T).\nThis paper is organized as follows. In Section 2 we recall the deﬁnition of ⊠q. In Section\n3 we describe how ⊠q is related to Uq(bsl2). In Section 4 we recall the basic theory of a\ndistance-regular graph Γ, focussing on the Q-polynomial property and the subconstituent\nalgebra. In Section 5 we discuss the split decomposition of Γ. In Section 6 we give our main\nresults.\nThroughout the paper C denotes the ﬁeld of complex numbers.\n2\nThe q-tetrahedron algebra ⊠q\nIn this section we recall the q-tetrahedron algebra. We ﬁx a nonzero scalar q ∈C such that\nq2 ̸= 1 and deﬁne\n[n]q = qn −q−n\nq −q−1 ,\nn = 0, 1, 2, . . .\nWe let Z4 = Z/4Z denote the cyclic group of order 4.\nDeﬁnition 2.1 [24, Deﬁnition 10.1] Let ⊠q denote the unital associative C-algebra that has\ngenerators\n{xij | i, j ∈Z4, j −i = 1 or j −i = 2}\nand the following relations:\n(i) For i, j ∈Z4 such that j −i = 2,\nxijxji = 1.\n2--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSome matrices associated with the split decomposition for a Q-polynomial distance-regular graph (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/0710.4383)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 19.1 years ago; Crawled: last month; Under this assumption Ito and Terwilliger displayed an action of the $q$q-tetrahedron algebra $\\boxtimes_q$⊠q​ on the standard module of $\\Gamma$Γ. ... @misc{kim2007matricesassociatedsplitdecomposition, title={Some matrices associated with the split decomposition for a Q-polynomial distance-regular graph}, author={Joohyung Kim}, year={2007}, eprint={0710.4383}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/0710.4383}, }\nSubmitted 24 Oct 2007\n\n# Some matrices associated with the split decomposition for a Q-polynomial distance-regular graph\n\nJK\n\nJoohyung Kim\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe consider a $Q$Q-polynomial distance-regular graph $\\Gamma$Γ with vertex set $X$X and diameter $D \\geq 3$D≥3. For $\\mu, \\nu \\in \\lbrace \\downarrow, \\uparrow \\rbrace$μ,ν∈{↓,↑} we define a direct sum decomposition of the standard module $V=\\C X$V=\\C X, called the $(\\mu,\\nu)$(μ,ν)--split decomposition. For this decomposition we compute the complex conjugate and transpose of the associated primitive idempotents. Now fix $b,\\beta \\in \\mathbb C$b,β∈C such that $b \\neq 1$b=1 and assume $\\Gamma$Γ has classical parameters $(D,b,\\alpha,\\beta)$(D,b,α,β) with $\\alpha = b-1$α=b−1. Under this assumption Ito and Terwilliger displayed an action of the $q$q-tetrahedron algebra $\\boxtimes_q$⊠q​ on the standard module of $\\Gamma$Γ. To describe this action they defined eight matrices in $\\hbox{Mat}_X(\\mathbb C)$Mat X​(C), called \\begin{eqnarray*} \\label{eq:list} A,\\quad A^*,\\quad B,\\quad B^*, \\quad K,\\quad K^*,\\quad \\Phi,\\quad \\Psi. \\end{eqnarray*} For each matrix in the above list we compute the transpose and complex conjugate. Using this information we compute the transpose and complex conjugate for each generator of $\\boxtimes_q$⊠q​ on $V$V.\n\nView PaperPDF\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{kim2007matricesassociatedsplitdecomposition, title={Some matrices associated with the split decomposition for a Q-polynomial distance-regular graph}, author={Joohyung Kim}, year={2007}, eprint={0710.4383}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/0710.4383}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA duality between pairs of split decompositions for a $Q$-polynomial distance-regular graph (https://arxiv.org/abs/0705.0167)\nciteturn0academia17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 19.5 years ago; Title: A duality between pairs of split decompositions for a $Q$-polynomial distance-regular graph\nAuthors: Joohyung Kim ... In this paper we show that the ($\\downarrow,\\downarrow$)--split decomposition and the ($\\uparrow,\\uparrow$)--split decomposition are dual with respect to the standard Hermitian form on $V$.\nTitle: A duality between pairs of split decompositions for a $Q$-polynomial distance-regular graph\nAuthors: Joohyung Kim\nDate: Tue May  1 20:12:21 2007\n\nLet $\\Gamma$ denote a $Q$-polynomial distance-regular graph with diameter $D \\geq 3$ and standard module $V$. Recently Ito and Terwilliger introduced four direct sum decompositions of $V$; we call these the $(\\mu,\\nu)$--{\\it split decompositions} of $V$, where $\\mu, \\nu \\in \\lbrace \\downarrow, \\uparrow \\rbrace$. In this paper we show that the ($\\downarrow,\\downarrow$)--split decomposition and the ($\\uparrow,\\uparrow$)--split decomposition are dual with respect to the standard Hermitian form on $V$. We also show that the ($\\downarrow,\\uparrow$)--split decomposition and the ($\\uparrow,\\downarrow$)--split decomposition are dual with respect to the standard Hermitian form on $V$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDistance-regular graphs of $q$-Racah type and the $q$-tetrahedron algebra (https://arxiv.org/abs/0708.1992)\nciteturn0academia18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 19.2 years ago; Title: Distance-regular graphs of $q$-Racah type and the $q$-tetrahedron algebra ... In this paper we discuss a relationship between the following two algebras: (i) the subconstituent algebra $T$ of a distance-regular graph that has $q$-Racah type; (ii) the $q$-tetrahedron algebra $\\boxtimes_q$ which is a $q$-deformation of the three-point $sl_2$ loop algebra.\nTitle: Distance-regular graphs of $q$-Racah type and the $q$-tetrahedron algebra\nAuthors: Tatsuro Ito, Paul Terwilliger\nDate: Wed Aug 15 06:03:43 2007\n\nIn this paper we discuss a relationship between the following two algebras: (i) the subconstituent algebra $T$ of a distance-regular graph that has $q$-Racah type; (ii) the $q$-tetrahedron algebra $\\boxtimes_q$ which is a $q$-deformation of the three-point $sl_2$ loop algebra. Assuming that every irreducible $T$-module is thin, we display an algebra homomorphism from $\\boxtimes_q$ into $T$ and show that $T$ is generated by the image together with the center $Z(T)$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDistance-regular graphs and the -tetrahedron algebra (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220296009_Distance-regular_graphs_and_the_-tetrahedron_algebra)\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 17.6 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; LetṼ µν i,j denote the orthogonal complement of V µν i−1,j + V µν i,j−1 in V µν i,j with respect to the standard Hermitian form. ... A duality between pairs of split decompositions for a Q-polynomial distance-regular graph ...   * Joohyung Kim ... In this paper we show that the ($\\downarrow,\\downarrow$)--split decomposition and the ($\\uparrow,\\uparrow$)--split decomposition are dual with respect to the standard Hermitian form on V.\n\n# Distance-regular graphs and the -tetrahedron algebra\n\n  * April 2009\n  * European Journal of Combinatorics 30(3):682-697\n\nDOI:10.1016/j.ejc.2008.07.011\n\n  * Source\n  * DBLP\n\nAuthors:\n\nTatsuro Ito\n\n  * Kanazawa University\n\nPaul Terwilliger\n\nPaul Terwilliger\n\n## Abstract\n\nLet Γ denote a distance-regular graph with classical parameters (D, b, α, β) and b ≠ 1, α = b - 1. The condition on α implies that Γ is formally self-dual. For b = q2 we use the adjacency matrix and dual adjacency matrix to obtain an action of the q-tetrahedron algebra {squared times}q on the standard module of Γ. We describe four algebra homomorphisms into {squared times}q from the quantum affine algebra Uq (over(s l, ̂)2); using these we pull back the above {squared times}q-action to obtain four actions of Uq (over(s l, ̂)2) on the standard module of Γ.\n\n\n## Citations (41)\n\n## References (54)\n\n... We call this form the standard Hermitian form on V . Recently Ito and Terwilliger introduced four direct sum decompositions of V [16]; we ca